//! check: fail(E0701)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: std.time, comptime.sandbox, comptime.sandbox.clock
//!
//! **D33, held as a rejection: no clock at compile time.** The capability
//! `std.time` carries (`Clock`, per I13) is refused categorically in the
//! comptime sandbox, and the sandbox table's reason is X12's: *two
//! identical builds must not observe different times*. Of the eight
//! categories this is the one whose refusal is purely about determinism —
//! nothing is confined, nothing leaks; a build that could read the clock
//! would simply stop being reproducible.
//!
//! The refused call is the BUILTIN and not the facade, for the reason
//! `tests/net/comptime_refuses.lu` records: the comptime engine has no
//! cross-module call at this pin, so a `comptime fn` naming any std
//! function is an honest `unsupported` at resolve before the sandbox ever
//! sees it (F-0051). The diagnostic is
//! `E0701: `time_now_ms` reaches the clock, which comptime code can never
//! touch`.
//!
//! `time.to_iso8601` below is called at RUN time and is pure — it is what
//! a comptime program COULD use the day the engine can call across a
//! module boundary, and it also keeps the import used (an unused import
//! is E0305).

use std.time

comptime fn probe() -> int {
    time_now_ms()
}

fn main() -> !int {
    const AT = probe()
    let stamp = time.to_iso8601(0)
    print("stamp: {stamp} at: {AT}")
    0
}
